summary.text:
This makes it clear:
- xclip does not fork — it stays in the foreground as the selection owner and prints "Waiting for selection requests, Control-C to quit".
- The reason it "worked" in the initial manual test was simply because the pipeline was backgrounded... no wait, in the initial manual test it wasn't backgrounded — `printf 'hello' | xclip -selection clipboard` was in the foreground... and yet the shell continued. Strange. Oh well — in the current evidence, when stdout is captured (not a tty), xclip stays in the foreground. In the initial manual test the stdout was... also captured by the shell tool (pipe). Hmm, but in that case the shell shouldn't have been able to continue past the pipeline...

Hmm wait, maybe in the initial test xclip actually did exit: because in that test Xvfb was started as `Xvfb :99 &` inside the same shell... the sequence: `Xvfb :99 ... & sleep 1.5; export DISPLAY=:99; printf 'hello' | xclip ...` — if xclip had blocked, nothing after that would have executed, and the entire thing would have hit the 30-second timeout. In the output, paste was displayed twice. Which means xclip must have exited there.

The difference between that run and now: hmm, in that run, xclip's stderr was the same pipe as stdout (the tool captures both). In this last run, xclip printed "Waiting for selection requests" — that goes to stderr/stdout. And it stayed alive.

The real difference: in the first manual test, I started Xvfb with `&` inside the shell. The xclip process... I can't fully explain this. Maybe in the first test xclip crashed/exited because Xvfb took a while to come up? No — paste succeeded with "hello", so the owner was alive at paste time (xclip -o fetched data from "someone"). If xclip had exited, its owner (the child of the fork?) would have served it. Hmm, wait — if xclip had forked: parent exits → shell continues; child stays as owner → serves the paste. This matches the first test perfectly. And the 30-second timeout kill at the end: Xvfb (backgrounded) was keeping the shell alive! Yes — `Xvfb :99 &` — the shell waits for all background jobs? No, a non-interactive bash exits even with background jobs running... actually bash exits immediately; the tool's 30-second timeout is just the tool's cap... but the output says "shell tool terminated command after exceeding timeout" — meaning the command was still running at the 30-second mark. If bash had exited, the command would be complete. Xvfb was a child of the shell's process group, still running → the tool kept waiting → timeout. That explains it.

So: in the first manual test xclip forked (stdout was... a pipe?). And in all my probe tests xclip did not fork. What's different???

Let me look at xclip's source. Debian bookworm's xclip is 0.13. Let me grab the source and find out when it forks. `apt-get source xclip` needs deb-src enabled. Instead let me download the tarball from the network... or more simply — read the xclip behavior: search for "Waiting for selection requests" in the source online? I know this string: it's printed by xclip in main() when !fork...

Let me recall xclip.c's main() (I've read this source before):

```c
int main (int argc, char *argv[]) {
    ...
    parse_args ...

    ...
    /* If we are not a terminal, fork */
    if (isatty (STDOUT_FILENO) == 0) {
        ...
    }
```

No, that's not right. Hmm.

Actually, here's xclip.c from the official site (xclip 0.13):

```c
    /* Get the selection from the user */
    ...
    if (keepalive) {
        /* ... */
    }
    ...
    /* Fork into the background */
    if (!foreground) {
        pid_t pid;
        pid = fork();
        if (pid == 0) {
            /* child process */
            signal (SIGHUP, SIG_IGN);
            ...
        } else if (pid > 0) {
            /* parent process */
            printf ("Waiting for selection requests, Control-C to quit\n");  ???
```

No, wait. "Waiting for selection requests, Control-C to quit" is printed by the foreground process (the parent) and it waits... hmm, in that case the parent stays.

Honestly, empirical data beats memory. Facts:
1. In this environment, when stdout is not a tty (pipe), xclip stays as the owner (no useful fork; at least the launched process keeps living).
2. Paste from another process works while the owner is alive (in the last test, "hello <- paste rc=0" was displayed while the xclip pipeline was running in the background).
3. When the owner dies, the clipboard content is lost (X selections are volatile).

Implications for the library: `copy_xclip` must not block forever in wait(). Options:

A) Use xclip with the `-f` flag? No wait, -f = "stay in foreground" — that's the opposite. Without -f... in the tests, even without -f it stays in the foreground. So in this xclip version, fork might never happen. Let me check what the flags mean in this version: `xclip --help`.

B) Don't wait() on the xclip process after copying. Just launch it, write, close stdin, and let it keep running (detached) as the owner. It will live until the selection is lost or the process dies. To avoid the zombie/leak, we could also not wait (the process outlives the parent python — when the parent python exits, xclip becomes an orphan owned by init; the selection lives until it's lost). For a library, this is actually the correct pattern: the clipboard owner must outlive the copy() call (the content must persist for other processes to paste it!).

Wait — that's the key insight: a clipboard tool must stay alive (in the background) as the selection owner. If we wait() for xclip, then as soon as it exits the clipboard is cleared (X selection model: when the owner dies, the selection becomes empty — unless a clipboard manager takes over). So:
- In a real desktop: a clipboard manager (or the persistent xclip process) holds the data. When the user copies with a text editor, the editor stays alive and serves the selection.
- xclip is designed to fork to the background and serve until the selection is lost.
- If xclip (the launched process) stays in the foreground and we wait() on it — it will never exit while it owns the selection → deadlock.

So the correct implementation of copy_xclip: Popen, write, close stdin, and don't wait (or wait briefly). The xclip process continues as the owner in the background.

But then, the "owner registration race": an immediate paste might race with the selection claim. Add a small sleep (e.g. 0.1s) after closing stdin.

But hmm — zombie processes: the xclip child is never waited on → when it finally exits (when the selection is lost, e.g. when a new copy replaces it — the old owner receives SelectionClear and exits), it becomes a zombie until the parent reaps it. If the parent python is alive, the zombie lingers (harmless, minor). When the parent dies, init reaps it. Acceptable. Alternative: launch with `start_new_session=True` + double... no, just not waiting is enough. Or: Popen with stdin from a temporary file? Hmm.

Actually, wait — I want to reconsider: does this xclip fork when stdout is a pipe? Evidence: no (poll is None even after 1–3 seconds, and the process shows up in ps). And in the first manual test, it clearly forked (the shell continued, and later the paste succeeded after the "pipeline" — hmm, did the pipeline complete? Actually in the first test, maybe xclip forked because stdout was a tty? The shell tool's capture — is that a pty or a pipe? If the tool uses a pty for the shell, then xclip's stdout (inherited) is a pty (tty) → xclip forks → parent exits → shell continues. In my python probes, stdout was an explicit PIPE (not a tty) → no fork. In the last manual test, the pipeline was explicitly backgrounded so I could observe. This is consistent: xclip forks only when stdout is a tty (isatty check).

Let me verify xclip's source to be sure — I'll grab it quickly from the Debian snapshot via apt-get source... actually more simply: `xclip --help` might document the behavior:

